WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.880
At least half of what presents

2
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.640
to all physical health doctors

3
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.610
arises from a mental health condition.

4
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.313
So much of the time it is trauma.

5
00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.690
Trauma negatively impacts all of us.

6
00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.810
My guest for this exploration into trauma

7
00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:20.880
is Dr. Paul Conti.

8
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.830
Trauma is something that leaves the brain

9
00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.960
different going forward.

10
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.620
A psychiatrist and expert in treating trauma,

11
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.447
Dr. Conti is also the author of

12
00:00:31.447 --> 00:00:33.747
"Trauma: The Invisible Epidemic:

13
00:00:33.747 --> 00:00:36.810
How Trauma Works and How We Can Heal From It"

14
00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.490
If we keep it inside of us,

15
00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.750
it is very, very toxic,

16
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.300
because we are doing the exact opposite

17
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.780
of what we need to do in order to heal.

18
00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:49.680
This conversation ranks, at least to my mind,

19
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.860
among the more important in the history of the show.

20
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.737
The whole foundational aspect of who we are

21
00:00:55.737 --> 00:00:59.430
and how we see ourselves becomes different.

22
00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.100
Please click that pesky subscribe button.

23
00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.102
And with that out of the way, please enjoy.

24
00:01:05.102 --> 00:01:07.769
(pensive music)

25
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.210
Super nice to meet you.

26
00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.043
I'm honored to meet you

27
00:01:19.043 --> 00:01:20.070
Thank you.
and to be able to share

28
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.893
this time and space with you this afternoon.

29
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.000
I've been over the many, you know,

30
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.900
kind of years endeavoring to understand

31
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.710
how trauma has played a crucial role, surprisingly,

32
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.933
in my many pathologies.

33
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.230
And it's been instrumental in informing

34
00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.720
how I've grappled with my own healing.

35
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.600
And the more, and I know you'll agree with me on this,

36
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.140
the more that I kind of learn and discover what trauma is,

37
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:49.973
what it's about,

38
00:01:49.973 --> 00:01:51.990
the more convinced I personally have become

39
00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.670
that it really does lay at the root

40
00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:56.580
of so much mental and emotional pathology.

41
00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.750
So...
Yes.

42
00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.640
You know, it's really great to be able

43
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.640
to kind of seize this opportunity to discuss

44
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.650
what I think is, you know,

45
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:07.200
not only a crucial aspect of what it means to live

46
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.100
an examined life,

47
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.620
but a piece that unfortunately

48
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.350
is really all too often overlooked,

49
00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.750
the "invisible epidemic," as you call it.

50
00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:16.950
Yes.

51
00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.467
You know, because I think trauma is something

52
00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:22.020
that we've all experienced to some degree.

53
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.840
I think it lives on a spectrum, much like addiction.

54
00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.000
Yes.
But the extent to which,

55
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.100
and the manner in which

56
00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.010
trauma impacts our lives downstream

57
00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.900
is absolutely monumental.

58
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:34.733
Right?

59
00:02:34.733 --> 00:02:36.720
Agree completely.
So in thinking about

60
00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.380
how to kind of enter into this conversation,

61
00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.830
in my mind, I'm sort of thinking of it in three parts,

62
00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.810
the first part being,

63
00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.730
defining what we mean about trauma,

64
00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.650
when we talk about trauma.

65
00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:52.170
Second, the many ways in which trauma

66
00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.760
manifests itself in our lives.

67
00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.050
And then third,

68
00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.080
the means by which we can effectively process, heal,

69
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.330
and even prevent trauma

70
00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.487
and those downstream effects.
Yes.

71
00:03:02.487 --> 00:03:05.610
So why don't we start with defining our terms here?

72
00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.975
Like what is trauma?

73
00:03:07.975 --> 00:03:09.720
That term gets thrown around

74
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:10.553
pretty cavalierly, right?
Right.

75
00:03:10.553 --> 00:03:12.540
So I think it's important that we understand

76
00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:13.560
what we're talking about.

77
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.550
Right, no, I agree.

78
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:15.500
I think we have to define it

79
00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:19.694
to have a meaningful conversation about it, absolutely.

80
00:03:19.694 --> 00:03:23.670
And I think for purposes of the book that I wrote

81
00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.130
and for conversations about trauma,

82
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.080
what I'm really interested in

83
00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.190
is trauma in the sense that something happens,

84
00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.310
which could be acute or could occur over time,

85
00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.340
that overwhelms our coping mechanisms

86
00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:41.610
and then leaves us different as we move forward.

87
00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:45.150
And that difference is rooted in brain biology.

88
00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.657
It's not a soft concept of,

89
00:03:46.657 --> 00:03:48.900
"Oh, I'm impacted by that because I remember it,"

90
00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.150
or, I mean, all those things can be true,

91
00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.390
but there's actually a change in the brain, right?

92
00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.960
And that change pushes some things

93
00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.540
that we know about ourselves, or know about the world,

94
00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:01.470
out of our mind.

95
00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.620
Like we forget things that we knew

96
00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:08.670
and we then ground to the world in a different way,

97
00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.260
in a way that comes more through

98
00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:13.950
the lens of vulnerability and vigilance.

99
00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:17.130
And we often do not know that those changes

100
00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.140
have happened inside of us.

101
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:22.350
So there are brain changes, there's psychological changes.

102
00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:24.660
They're real, they're identifiable.

103
00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.100
And the reason, as you talked about,

104
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.870
there can be so much downstream effect

105
00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:31.620
is if it's impacting us so deeply and we don't know it,

106
00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.780
then how do we stave off the sort of

107
00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.613
domino effect of consequences?

108
00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.870
Yeah, in trying to differentiate or distinguish

109
00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:43.500
between a difficult experience or a painful experience

110
00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.730
and something that would be categorized as trauma,

111
00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:51.480
there seems to be a concretized narrative

112
00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:52.650
that ensues, right?

113
00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:54.330
There's a storytelling piece

114
00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:57.180
that gets kind of instilled within us

115
00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.040
that becomes very difficult to break,

116
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.040
like a looping that then kind of drives behavior.

117
00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.480
Right, it's a change in life narrative.

118
00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:07.830
So life narrative, and like,

119
00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.240
what do I think about myself,

120
00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:11.370
and what do I think about my life history?

121
00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.170
So it's retroactive, right?

122
00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.120
So the change in life narrative

123
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.580
is not just going forward, right?

124
00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.550
But it's going forward as linked to the past.

125
00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.500
And it very often changes

126
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:23.850
what we've thought about ourselves.

127
00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:26.757
So the whole foundational aspect of who we are

128
00:05:26.757 --> 00:05:30.690
and how we see ourselves becomes different.

129
00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.900
Yeah, talk a little bit more about that, how the timeline,

130
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.660
it pervades the timeline in both directions,

131
00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:38.610
and actually, as a result,

132
00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.740
prevents us from actually being present

133
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.570
in what is truly real.

134
00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:43.500
Right, right.

135
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.050
I think the best example I can give of this,

136
00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:47.790
which I write about to some degree in the book,

137
00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:51.060
is the loss of my youngest brother to suicide.

138
00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.670
And, you know, it was a much earlier stage in my life,

139
00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:56.460
long before I went to medical school,

140
00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:01.140
and my whole view of myself, and of life,

141
00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:04.320
and what the world could hold for me was different.

142
00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.410
Now, at some point in time, I became aware of it, right?

143
00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:09.150
But it was a whole point,

144
00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:11.220
a whole space of time where I wasn't,

145
00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.410
that instead of feeling like, look,

146
00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.420
I'm a pretty smart guy, and I work hard,

147
00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:17.010
and I think I can make my way in the world,

148
00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.720
and like, I've achieved some things,

149
00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.570
which kind of tells me that I can achieve more things,

150
00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:23.430
even though in one's early twenties,

151
00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:24.263
it can be so daunting, right?

152
00:06:24.263 --> 00:06:26.160
Like, how am I gonna get where I want to go?

153
00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:28.860
But I have faith in myself because I've gotten places.

154
00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.720
And there's a whole conception of self

155
00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.300
as being able to navigate the world.

156
00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:34.800
And after that,

157
00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.830
I thought of myself very differently in a way that was,

158
00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.480
oh, like I'm cursed, right?

159
00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:40.770
Or my family's cursed,

160
00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:43.144
and like nothing really ever works out, right?

161
00:06:43.144 --> 00:06:43.977
And like, you know,

162
00:06:43.977 --> 00:06:46.290
you can try and you can work hard, but like,

163
00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:47.550
what does it really get you, you know?

164
00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:51.090
And then I felt very defensive, and beleaguered,

165
00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:54.780
and vulnerable, and there was a whole change in habits,

166
00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:55.980
or like, my mood was different,

167
00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.050
my level of anxiety was different.

168
00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.440
My choices about who to spend time with were different,

169
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:02.880
whether it's friends or dating,

170
00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.180
like it was a shift towards the unhealthy, right?

171
00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.070
Without a realization like,

172
00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.250
hey, you don't actually think this way about yourself

173
00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:13.080
in the world, or at least you didn't before.

174
00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.960
And it was very much a shock to me

175
00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:18.420
to have that realization that like,

176
00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:19.980
oh, like this made everything different,

177
00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.250
not just going forward, but going backwards, too.

178
00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:24.270
Hm.

179
00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:28.290
What was the journey towards that self-realization like?

180
00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:31.590
Like there was a period of time in which you didn't have

181
00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:34.567
the awareness to understand that you were operating under,

182
00:07:34.567 --> 00:07:37.080
you know, the sort of downstream implications

183
00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:37.913
of this trauma,

184
00:07:37.913 --> 00:07:39.090
you were just living your life,

185
00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:41.550
and then something changed where you were able to see it

186
00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:43.800
and evaluate it more objectively.

187
00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:46.140
Yeah, a couple of different factors.

188
00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:49.510
You know, one was I was aware of

189
00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.980
how I was sort of miserable in ways

190
00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:56.370
that weren't just related to the loss of my brother.

191
00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:58.830
Like, you know, there was that sort of hanging over me,

192
00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:01.080
but, you know, I was drinking too much

193
00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.550
and hanging out with unhealthy people,

194
00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:06.810
and not thinking ahead to like what I wanted next, right?

195
00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:09.687
And so realizing that like,

196
00:08:09.687 --> 00:08:11.340
oh, I'm pretty miserable, right?

197
00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:14.103
Like I'm not sleeping as well, my mood is low,

198
00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:16.740
I have a negative view of everything.

199
00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.440
Like, oh, like here's some new project at work.

200
00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.300
I had a different career at the time,

201
00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.250
and instead of thinking like, oh, I can do this well,

202
00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:24.180
I'm like, where's that gonna lead?

203
00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:26.010
I was like, oh, what's gonna go wrong, you know?

204
00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:29.760
Yeah.
And it was very clear

205
00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:30.720
the change in myself,

206
00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.950
which was a change towards limitation and misery, right?

207
00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:38.190
And then I also got a little bit of psychotherapy, you know,

208
00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:40.350
I'd come from a place, so to speak,

209
00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.510
where people didn't get psychotherapy, right?

210
00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:43.370
And it was thought that, oh,

211
00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:44.910
if you're getting mental health care, like,

212
00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:46.920
that was for people who were really sick, right?

213
00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.410
So I didn't have this understanding that

214
00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:51.030
no, this is a tool that we can use

215
00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.340
to make our lives so much better.

216
00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.890
And I accessed just like 10 sessions or so

217
00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:58.410
through my insurance through work,

218
00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:00.330
and they were so elucidating, you know,

219
00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:03.607
to sit with somebody who then was able to say like,

220
00:09:03.607 --> 00:09:06.180
"Right, you lost your brother, and that's a problem,

221
00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.000
of course, but there's all these other problems now

222
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.740
that are in you," right?

223
00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:12.300
And that really opened,

224
00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.220
I mean, it did really open my eyes.

225
00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.950
And in looking back at that experience,

226
00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:18.600
do you have a sense of

227
00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:23.140
how much of your reaction to your brother's suicide

228
00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:28.830
was driven by just your internal mechanisms?

229
00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.710
Or was there a piece where

230
00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:33.810
the way that you were parented through that

231
00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:35.010
had an implication as well?

232
00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.470
I mean, I want to get into kind of generational aspects

233
00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:38.303
of this later,

234
00:09:38.303 --> 00:09:40.380
but in looking at like,

235
00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.320
how did your parents weather that experience,

236
00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.630
and how did they communicate to you

237
00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:46.770
about that at the time,

238
00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:50.370
and did that have some implications

239
00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:52.350
in terms of how you responded?

240
00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:53.700
Yeah.

241
00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:55.320
I think we all, just in a sense,

242
00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:59.640
slotted into what was the societal norm then,

243
00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:03.870
which was not talking about it, not processing it, right?

244
00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:08.490
Not communicating about really deep changes, right?

245
00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:10.920
The idea that, well, what good would that do, right?

246
00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.690
Like, all that's gonna do is make things worse,

247
00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:14.890
which of course the opposite is true, right?

248
00:10:15.858 --> 00:10:19.740
So I think that my parents and the people around me

249
00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.350
who wished, of course, to help me and themselves,

250
00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:26.310
didn't understand that our reaction to trauma,

251
00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:31.140
so the reflexive guilt and shame, plus the brain changes,

252
00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:33.720
which is all kind of part and parcel of the same thing,

253
00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.130
drives us inside.

254
00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:37.530
And the way we often reflexively handle it,

255
00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:38.910
well, this is something to be ashamed of.

256
00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:40.300
I mean, so the thought that,

257
00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:42.690
well, like I'm cursed, or my family's cursed,

258
00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:44.160
and like nothing good can come of it,

259
00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:46.710
that's not something people generally feel proud of, right?

260
00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:49.080
So you wanna hide that inside.

261
00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:52.350
And then, you know, so many people were suffering,

262
00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:54.540
but in ways that were isolated,

263
00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:56.760
and isolated from one another.

264
00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:59.610
And I think that that took a very big toll.

265
00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:01.320
I think it took a very big toll, for example,

266
00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:02.700
on my mother, who then, I think,

267
00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.360
was depressed much of the rest of her life,

268
00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.960
which I think predisposed to some very bad health outcomes.

269
00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.380
Like, I think where that goes, if we don't revisit that,

270
00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:17.700
if guilt, shame, fear drives us inside of ourselves

271
00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:20.733
and we live that new reality,

272
00:11:20.733 --> 00:11:22.830
that that often is the story, that it's like,

273
00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.620
oh, it's years of suffering and then an early death.

274
00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:28.790
Like, I think that that often is the outcome of it,

275
00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:30.780
or it might not be years of suffering,

276
00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:32.250
'cause people might accelerate themselves

277
00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.830
towards an early death, but like,

278
00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:35.970
that's the truth of it.

279
00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:39.090
If we keep it inside of us, it is very, very toxic,

280
00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:42.720
because we are doing literally the exact opposite

281
00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.570
of what we need to do in order to heal.

282
00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:48.510
Sure, so this is the major key in the whole thing,

283
00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:51.990
and why this is such an invisible epidemic,

284
00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:53.670
because, and I know you talked about this

285
00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.070
with our mutual friend, Dr. Huberman.

286
00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:58.620
Yes.
But the natural inclination

287
00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:03.000
when we suffer through or survive a traumatic experience

288
00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:06.330
is to compartmentalize it, to repress it.

289
00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:08.940
And, you know, Dr. Huberman characterized that

290
00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.200
as maladaptive,

291
00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.630
but it's really a survival mechanism.

292
00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:14.340
For some reason or another,

293
00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:15.780
this is our instinct

294
00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:19.020
in how to best weather these experiences.

295
00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:22.380
So talk a little bit about that and how that kind of

296
00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:25.050
drives these negative outcomes over time.

297
00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:25.883
Right.

298
00:12:25.883 --> 00:12:26.910
And certainly I'm not an expert

299
00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:29.790
on psychological anthropology, so to speak,

300
00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:33.660
but I think if we look at what human history was like

301
00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:36.900
throughout most of human history, it makes sense, right?

302
00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:40.050
The response in us to compartmentalize

303
00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:41.970
and to hide something away with us

304
00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.000
probably makes sense in a time period

305
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.190
where we just didn't live that long, right?

306
00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.080
So, you know, if you're out on a hunt

307
00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:52.170
and like you have to get some food, right?

308
00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:53.520
And there's a bad outcome

309
00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:57.420
and somebody in the party is killed in front of you, right?

310
00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.183
Well, you still have to find food, right?

311
00:12:59.183 --> 00:13:01.800
I mean, there's a survival advantage

312
00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.490
to being able to compartmentalize in the short term,

313
00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.490
in the service of immediate needs, right?

314
00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:12.210
Which doesn't fit at all with how we live our lives

315
00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:13.230
in the modern world,

316
00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.480
where like where we go and what we do

317
00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.300
is much more based upon our conception of self, right?

318
00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:19.620
And we're not, you know,

319
00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.505
we're not living lives that are limited into,

320
00:13:22.505 --> 00:13:24.360
hey, if you can get to your early twenties and reproduce,

321
00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.040
like we call that good,

322
00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:27.120
and if you make it further than that,

323
00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:28.133
you become an elder, right?
Right.

324
00:13:28.133 --> 00:13:31.020
I mean, if it's not like that,

325
00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:34.320
then these reflexive mechanisms in us

326
00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:36.233
designed to keep us going, right,

327
00:13:36.233 --> 00:13:38.100
they're not adaptive anymore,

328
00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:39.390
because we're not just looking

329
00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:41.880
at a short-term survival picture,

330
00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:44.040
but we're looking at a long-term picture of like,

331
00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:45.510
how are you gonna live your life?

332
00:13:45.510 --> 00:13:47.400
How are you gonna thrive?

333
00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:48.930
How are you gonna strive?

334
00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:51.100
And that's very, very different

335
00:13:52.050 --> 00:13:55.170
because it must be in us this strongly

336
00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:56.523
because of something adaptive, right?

337
00:13:56.523 --> 00:13:59.010
Like the reflex of guilt and shame and hiding it,

338
00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:00.750
it can't be from nowhere.

339
00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:03.780
It must serve an adaptive evolutionary purpose.

340
00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:05.100
Right.

341
00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:07.050
But that doesn't mean that it serves a purpose

342
00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:08.700
in the world we live in now.

343
00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:09.533
Right.

344
00:14:09.533 --> 00:14:11.640
In other words, in an era in which

345
00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.770
we weren't expected to live past 30

346
00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:16.470
and all we're here to do is reproduce,

347
00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:20.130
push it down, survive, get through the next day, reproduce,

348
00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:22.300
and then who cares what happens?

349
00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:24.390
Yeah, and that would predispose

350
00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:27.630
to a tremendous salience to the negative, right?

351
00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.460
So the example I'll give sometime is,

352
00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:32.910
imagine if in the hunter-gatherer era of humankind,

353
00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.460
if you're hungry and you're looking for something to eat,

354
00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:37.920
and you find a berry and it looks like it might be good,

355
00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:40.650
and it actually is, and it's nutritious and it's helpful.

356
00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:42.480
It's good to remember that, right?

357
00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.630
But if you find a berry and you get deathly ill,

358
00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:48.120
you better not forget that.

359
00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:50.460
So the salience of the negative

360
00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:52.980
also has a survival advantage,

361
00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:55.770
but we build stories around that

362
00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:59.970
in the way we live modern life that just shut us down.

363
00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:02.220
So the example, I mean, it's so sad,

364
00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:04.140
I can't possibly count how many times

365
00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:06.330
I've talked to someone in my clinical work

366
00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:09.330
who's been, say, a victim of a sexual assault,

367
00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:11.247
has been maybe attacked somewhere,

368
00:15:11.247 --> 00:15:12.900
and then takes away from that

369
00:15:12.900 --> 00:15:15.360
the lessons of guilt, and shame, and vulnerability,

370
00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:17.670
and like, I'm not safe out in the world.

371
00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.920
And now that that person doesn't wanna leave their house,

372
00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:22.710
or they don't wanna go out and live their lives,

373
00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:26.070
because there's something that tells them that,

374
00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:28.200
hey, you shouldn't do that, it's not safe to do that,

375
00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:29.310
and look, you couldn't keep yourself safe.

376
00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:32.310
And then all the guilt and shame comes around that.

377
00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:36.450
And it's so shockingly maladaptive,

378
00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:39.480
but the person can't see that if they're sort of

379
00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.930
in the throes of the reflexive survival urges.

380
00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:44.250
It tells them, right,

381
00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.230
blaming yourself and never leaving the house

382
00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:49.439
sounds like the best way to survive, right?

383
00:15:49.439 --> 00:15:50.910
But it's not.
Yeah, the,

384
00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:55.290
the fear and the sense that the world is an unsafe place

385
00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:57.960
seem like self-evident ideas.

386
00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:00.030
The guilt and the shame, however,

387
00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:03.030
are a little bit more challenging to understand.

388
00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:06.120
Like if somebody has suffered, you know,

389
00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:09.183
is victimized by some act of violence,

390
00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:13.110
it does strike one as unusual that that person

391
00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:15.270
would then feel ashamed of that or guilty,

392
00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:16.950
but these two emotions,

393
00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:19.920
and I'd like you to distinguish between the two,

394
00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:22.770
seem to go hand in hand with trauma

395
00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:26.130
and the kind of attempt to survive it.

396
00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:28.200
Right, I think the guilt and shame part

397
00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:30.900
is much more surprising, right?

398
00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:33.930
And as I came to realize over years

399
00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:36.420
of just doing clinical work, like, wait a second,

400
00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:38.700
like I'm seeing this writ large,

401
00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:41.160
across the vast majority of problems.

402
00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:43.350
So the vast majority of problems

403
00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:44.580
that I'm seeing and treating,

404
00:16:44.580 --> 00:16:46.707
and the vast majority of problems in myself,

405
00:16:46.707 --> 00:16:49.230
are being driven by trauma

406
00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:52.710
and the power of the reflexive guilt and shame,

407
00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:55.800
which doesn't make the obvious sense that fear does.

408
00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:57.777
And again, we don't know why that is,

409
00:16:57.777 --> 00:16:59.970
but my thought about that is, you know,

410
00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:03.180
neurobiologically, there are different

411
00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:04.137
parsed out definitions, right,

412
00:17:04.137 --> 00:17:07.560
and the idea that affect is something that's aroused in us,

413
00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:09.990
that's created in us without our choice,

414
00:17:09.990 --> 00:17:14.040
and affects very powerfully modulate our behavior.

415
00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:16.170
So like an affect of fear, you know,

416
00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:17.970
starts blood coursing through the body,

417
00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:19.590
and our heart's racing more

418
00:17:19.590 --> 00:17:20.880
and we're ready for fight or flight,

419
00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:22.800
and then we know it, right,

420
00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:24.750
because it's much more important

421
00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:26.280
to prepare the mind and body

422
00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:28.710
than it is to have conscious awareness of it.

423
00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:32.910
So aroused affects drive us very strongly,

424
00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.580
and shame seems to be among

425
00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:39.030
the strongest modulators of behavior.

426
00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:40.350
You know, imagine as an adult,

427
00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:43.500
where, you know, I try to avoid this assiduously

428
00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:45.420
because it feels so bad,

429
00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:48.030
but where I do something that I feel ashamed of.

430
00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:49.590
It's like the worst feeling, right?

431
00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:50.787
It's, at least, I think for me,

432
00:17:50.787 --> 00:17:52.590
and a lot of people, I think, describe this,

433
00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:53.760
like, it's worse than being angry.

434
00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:55.410
It's worse than being afraid.

435
00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:56.970
Like, it's this shame in me that like,

436
00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:59.700
I've done something and it feels so bad.

437
00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:02.850
And I think it's such a strong behavior modulator

438
00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:07.020
that if you add shame to fear or to anger,

439
00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:10.860
then you have a very powerful behavioral modulator

440
00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:13.920
against, you know, the breadth of perspective

441
00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:16.830
that's exactly what we want to thrive in the world.

442
00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:18.030
It says shut down your life,

443
00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:19.677
shut down your perspective.

444
00:18:19.677 --> 00:18:21.567
And so that's my thought about it.

445
00:18:21.567 --> 00:18:24.120
And again, I can't know for sure,

446
00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:26.370
but I think that's why shame is so powerfully

447
00:18:26.370 --> 00:18:27.203
in the picture.

448
00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:32.580
Shame seems to be one of the most powerful drivers

449
00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:36.660
of unhealthy human behavior in so many ways.

450
00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:39.180
And I tend to look at things like this

451
00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:42.570
through the lens of addiction and sobriety,

452
00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:44.460
like we were chatting before the podcast,

453
00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:45.720
you live in Portland.

454
00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.810
Portland is where my sober journey began.

455
00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:50.160
I was in a treatment center there

456
00:18:50.160 --> 00:18:54.480
for quite some time in the late 90s.

457
00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:57.990
And that was the inception of me

458
00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:02.340
confronting my own shame of my addictive behaviors

459
00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:04.410
and the secrets that I was keeping.

460
00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:08.613
And over many years and, you know, thousands of AA meetings,

461
00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:13.830
one of the key kind of things that I've learned,

462
00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:14.730
that I've taken to heart through

463
00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:18.270
this experience of being sober,

464
00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:22.810
is the extent to which 12-step and AA

465
00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:28.050
are able to normalize trauma

466
00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:31.140
to eradicate that shame association,

467
00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:32.220
because you're sort of compelled

468
00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:34.230
to get up in front of a group of people

469
00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:36.720
and share your story, warts and all.

470
00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:41.720
And the liberating impact of seeing somebody else do that,

471
00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:47.220
who can own their past without that shame instinct,

472
00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:51.000
gives another person permission to, you know,

473
00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:52.500
walk that same path.

474
00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:53.730
Yes.
And there's something

475
00:19:53.730 --> 00:19:57.570
about freeing yourself from that association that

476
00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:00.107
then opens up unlimited possibilities

477
00:20:00.107 --> 00:20:01.710
for healing and growth,
Yes.

478
00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.420
but it's so difficult.

479
00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:04.440
We're so locked up.

480
00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:06.960
We create these prisons around these secrets

481
00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:08.223
that we hold.
Yes.

482
00:20:08.223 --> 00:20:10.590
Yes, and I think what you're describing

483
00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:14.910
is that it becomes then permissive to share

484
00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:17.640
and to say like, this is my truth, this is my reality,

485
00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:19.563
but I think that's retrospective too, right?

486
00:20:19.563 --> 00:20:23.700
That it becomes permissive to have had things happen

487
00:20:23.700 --> 00:20:26.670
or to have done things that have aroused shame, right?

488
00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:30.960
It ceases then to be this internal litmus test on

489
00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:32.827
am I a good person, am I a worthwhile person?

490
00:20:32.827 --> 00:20:35.910
You know, so much of that I think comes from the shame

491
00:20:35.910 --> 00:20:39.330
that's aroused in the moments over things we do

492
00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:40.800
that we don't feel great about.

493
00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:43.590
And it makes a story inside of us that says like,

494
00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:46.080
look, that's not okay and you're not okay, right?

495
00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:49.950
So to have it be permissive to be validated, like,

496
00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:54.060
look, this stuff can have happened,

497
00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:57.000
and it's not a litmus test on who I am, right?

498
00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:01.080
I can define who I am now going forward.

499
00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:04.080
And I think that's why when AA and 12-step

500
00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:05.820
works really well,

501
00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:07.890
I think among its most powerful,

502
00:21:07.890 --> 00:21:09.540
if not maybe its most powerful,

503
00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:11.883
impact is anti-shame.

504
00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:16.620
Yeah, I think of the opposite, or the antidote,

505
00:21:16.620 --> 00:21:19.020
to shame being vulnerability,

506
00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:20.940
and a close cousin of that is courage,

507
00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:24.570
because it requires courage to be vulnerable.

508
00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:28.590
The adage "shame can't survive the light" is very true.

509
00:21:28.590 --> 00:21:31.170
But in order to move towards that light,

510
00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:33.990
you have to summon the courage to be vulnerable.

511
00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:36.090
And in the sharing of that,

512
00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:38.130
there is this unbelievable release

513
00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:41.490
that's, quite frankly, astonishing.

514
00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:43.080
It's like having a two ton weight

515
00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:45.003
lifted off your shoulders.
Yes, yes.

516
00:21:45.003 --> 00:21:48.000
And then in the wake of that, realizing,

517
00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:49.620
like why did I, you know,

518
00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:51.840
haul that thing around for so long?

519
00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:53.610
Yes, yes.

520
00:21:53.610 --> 00:21:55.380
I agree completely.

521
00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:59.520
I think shame is so toxic

522
00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:06.060
and when we sort of set that down inside of us,

523
00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:11.060
we start making like immutable judgements about ourselves

524
00:22:11.430 --> 00:22:14.283
and they take away our sense of agency,

525
00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:17.730
our autonomy, our free will, right?

526
00:22:17.730 --> 00:22:20.340
So, you know, at times I've thought or said

