00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:20.610
right, if awful things happen to me, I can survive them.

786
00:33:20.610 --> 00:33:22.440
But inadvertently what we often do

787
00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:25.620
is just bring more, and more, and more trauma,

788
00:33:25.620 --> 00:33:28.680
and more, and more, and more lessons, so to speak, that say,

789
00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:31.440
hey, you just can't keep yourself safe in this world.

790
00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:34.620
Yeah, I mean, there is a neurochemical allure

791
00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:36.450
to that type of behavior, right?

792
00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:37.283
Like the doom scrolling,
Yes.

793
00:33:37.283 --> 00:33:38.550
Yes.
and you just can't

794
00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:40.800
put it down, or you can't turn off the news

795
00:33:40.800 --> 00:33:43.290
in the background, it's gotta be on all the time.

796
00:33:43.290 --> 00:33:45.120
And even intellectually understanding

797
00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:46.380
that this is not good for you

798
00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:49.920
and it's making you progressively more and more unhappy,

799
00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:53.430
there is an inability to course correct that behavior.

800
00:33:53.430 --> 00:33:54.990
Right, absolutely.

801
00:33:54.990 --> 00:33:57.660
The same way this happens in a lot of people,

802
00:33:57.660 --> 00:34:01.440
where if there's something a person's really worried about,

803
00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:04.620
the person keeps themselves thinking about it, right?

804
00:34:04.620 --> 00:34:06.840
And we do that because there's a superstition

805
00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:09.540
sort of built into the species that says,

806
00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:10.950
if I'm really worried about something,

807
00:34:10.950 --> 00:34:12.000
but I'm thinking about it,

808
00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:14.490
and I'm being vigilant about it in my thoughts,

809
00:34:14.490 --> 00:34:17.160
it's likely to stave it off, right?

810
00:34:17.160 --> 00:34:19.260
If there's a cancer I'm really worried about,

811
00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:20.093
because my goodness,

812
00:34:20.093 --> 00:34:23.070
a couple people in my family have had the cancer,

813
00:34:23.070 --> 00:34:24.540
then somewhere, it occurs to me

814
00:34:24.540 --> 00:34:26.070
that if I think about that,

815
00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:28.620
it's not gonna sneak up and get me, right?

816
00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:30.300
And like, that's a human reflex

817
00:34:30.300 --> 00:34:32.610
that probably comes from way back when,

818
00:34:32.610 --> 00:34:34.200
of like, if you're worried about something, think about it,

819
00:34:34.200 --> 00:34:35.160
it'll help keep you safe.

820
00:34:35.160 --> 00:34:37.320
But it doesn't serve us anymore.

821
00:34:37.320 --> 00:34:40.620
And then that's kind of the neurobiological function

822
00:34:40.620 --> 00:34:43.470
that's served by the doom scrolling or the, you know,

823
00:34:43.470 --> 00:34:45.360
always have the news on in the background,

824
00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:47.550
of like, I'm being vigilant, I'm aware,

825
00:34:47.550 --> 00:34:49.140
not just in terms of what is the news,

826
00:34:49.140 --> 00:34:50.820
but I'm like, it's in my mind every moment.

827
00:34:50.820 --> 00:34:53.217
And like, somehow that's gonna keep me safe.

828
00:34:53.217 --> 00:34:54.780
And it doesn't keep us safe.

829
00:34:54.780 --> 00:34:58.350
I mean, it does quite the opposite to us.

830
00:34:58.350 --> 00:34:59.610
Yeah, there's something about it

831
00:34:59.610 --> 00:35:02.700
that's wed to an illusion of being able

832
00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:05.250
to control outcomes in the future.

833
00:35:05.250 --> 00:35:08.460
But there's another piece that I think is important,

834
00:35:08.460 --> 00:35:10.890
which is about rewriting the past, right?

835
00:35:10.890 --> 00:35:13.770
If you have suffered some event in the past,

836
00:35:13.770 --> 00:35:15.510
that kind of obsession,

837
00:35:15.510 --> 00:35:18.990
the looping mind on the facts of that scenario

838
00:35:18.990 --> 00:35:22.920
is an attempt to reconcile that event and perhaps, you know,

839
00:35:22.920 --> 00:35:26.370
figure out how to make it different,

840
00:35:26.370 --> 00:35:29.700
or could it have been different had I done X, Y, or Z?

841
00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:30.540
Yes.

842
00:35:30.540 --> 00:35:32.460
It's great that you're bringing that up,

843
00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:35.310
because I think that's absolutely true.

844
00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:39.420
And it is so poorly served by just having it

845
00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:42.360
go over and over again in our minds, right?

846
00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:45.690
We rarely figure out something new, right?

847
00:35:45.690 --> 00:35:49.320
If you've been ruminating about something bad in the past

848
00:35:49.320 --> 00:35:51.540
over, and over, and over, and over again,

849
00:35:51.540 --> 00:35:54.540
what are the chances of thinking something new?

850
00:35:54.540 --> 00:35:57.960
Which is why sometimes a person will come in

851
00:35:57.960 --> 00:35:59.430
and they're willing to talk about trauma

852
00:35:59.430 --> 00:36:01.200
they haven't talked about before,

853
00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:03.390
and maybe I know this, like it's been telegraphed,

854
00:36:03.390 --> 00:36:04.620
but I haven't seen the person before,

855
00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:06.840
we haven't talked about the trauma before,

856
00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:09.000
and then they'll start talking about it.

857
00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:13.380
And sometimes I say almost nothing, right?

858
00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:15.990
I'm just, I'm a listening presence,

859
00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:18.450
and I'm reassuring, and encouraging,

860
00:36:18.450 --> 00:36:19.920
and whatever, you know, "mm-hm,"

861
00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:21.120
or whatever sounds I may make,

862
00:36:21.120 --> 00:36:24.277
and then the person at the end will think,

863
00:36:24.277 --> 00:36:26.580
"Oh my God, that was, I can't believe it.

864
00:36:26.580 --> 00:36:28.260
I can't believe how much better I feel."

865
00:36:28.260 --> 00:36:30.270
It's like, I didn't do anything, right?

866
00:36:30.270 --> 00:36:32.460
But the person put words to it,

867
00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:35.190
said it out loud to another person,

868
00:36:35.190 --> 00:36:38.070
and therefore was able to think about it in a different way.

869
00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:40.830
So the thousands of times it goes over in the brain,

870
00:36:40.830 --> 00:36:43.140
we very rarely learn something new.

871
00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:45.090
But if we put words to it,

872
00:36:45.090 --> 00:36:47.250
there are different parts of our brain that come online.

873
00:36:47.250 --> 00:36:48.083
There's a part of our brain

874
00:36:48.083 --> 00:36:50.430
called the ventromedial prefrontal cortex

875
00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:52.290
that's involved in reversal learning,

876
00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:55.500
so unlearning things we've learned that are wrong,

877
00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:56.333
for example.

878
00:36:56.333 --> 00:37:00.300
And we bring that much more online in the spoken word

879
00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:03.600
and with a witness, somebody hearing our words.

880
00:37:03.600 --> 00:37:06.540
So I think what you're saying is so important,

881
00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:07.950
but it's hard to achieve that

882
00:37:07.950 --> 00:37:10.770
when it's just running over and over again in our minds,

883
00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:12.210
'cause it just becomes often

884
00:37:12.210 --> 00:37:14.700
the same oppressive self narrative.

885
00:37:14.700 --> 00:37:16.353
Yeah, yeah.

886
00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:20.557
It's its own living pernicious entity, right?

887
00:37:20.557 --> 00:37:22.380
Yes.
Like we all know people

888
00:37:22.380 --> 00:37:25.560
who walk around with a rain cloud over their head

889
00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:28.710
and, you know, I'm fat, I'm ugly, I always get fired,

890
00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:30.180
and no matter what you say
Yes.

891
00:37:30.180 --> 00:37:31.080
to that person,

892
00:37:31.080 --> 00:37:35.130
it's impossible to kind of trigger them out of that mindset,

893
00:37:35.130 --> 00:37:36.660
which isn't rooted in reality,

894
00:37:36.660 --> 00:37:37.980
but obviously, you know,

895
00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:40.950
basically predicts all their life outcomes,

896
00:37:40.950 --> 00:37:42.810
because that's the way they're navigating the world,

897
00:37:42.810 --> 00:37:45.240
that's the way that they're interacting with other people,

898
00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:47.610
and of course that's gonna provoke the result

899
00:37:47.610 --> 00:37:50.280
that they seem not to desire,

900
00:37:50.280 --> 00:37:53.490
but which they continue to manifest time and time again.

901
00:37:53.490 --> 00:37:56.550
Right, the enaction, like E-N, not I-N, right,

902
00:37:56.550 --> 00:37:59.400
the enaction of a self-fulfilling prophecy

903
00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:03.210
is used as evidence that the prophecy was unavoidable.

904
00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:06.450
And boy, that's hard to get out of,

905
00:38:06.450 --> 00:38:07.303
but we can get out of it.

906
00:38:07.303 --> 00:38:09.540
And I know it was the sort of third topic of like,

907
00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:11.520
how do we get better, right?

908
00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:12.830
But, looking ahead a little bit to that

909
00:38:12.830 --> 00:38:14.010
is like how we get better

910
00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:17.700
is we stop the cycles that run over, and over,

911
00:38:17.700 --> 00:38:20.100
and over again in our minds

912
00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:23.520
by doing something different, by putting words to something,

913
00:38:23.520 --> 00:38:26.700
whether that's writing, it's speaking to someone trusted,

914
00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:28.200
it's going to psychotherapy,

915
00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:30.330
it's the thought of like, look this, you know,

916
00:38:30.330 --> 00:38:32.880
this over, and over, and over, and over again,

917
00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:34.950
that all it does is spin off misery

918
00:38:34.950 --> 00:38:36.360
and spin off negative symptoms

919
00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:38.360
and impacts us in mind and body, right?

920
00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:39.560
It impacts our immune system.

921
00:38:39.560 --> 00:38:41.730
It impacts our cardiovascular health.

922
00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:43.590
So all of this negativity,

923
00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:45.750
if we see all that's going on in me

924
00:38:45.750 --> 00:38:46.583
is just spinning that off,

925
00:38:46.583 --> 00:38:50.100
then like it's time for me to do something different, right?

926
00:38:50.100 --> 00:38:53.190
That's when, not just like, oh, things can get better,

927
00:38:53.190 --> 00:38:54.840
but things can get dramatically better,

928
00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:56.820
and sometimes dramatically better

929
00:38:56.820 --> 00:38:59.130
much more quickly than one might imagine,

930
00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:00.390
which kind of does make sense,

931
00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:02.040
'cause now something different is happening

932
00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:06.420
that actually serves a solution state to the problems

933
00:39:06.420 --> 00:39:08.370
that have just been running over and over again

934
00:39:08.370 --> 00:39:09.670
and spinning off symptoms.

935
00:39:10.770 --> 00:39:13.950
I wanna go back to the chronic trauma piece

936
00:39:13.950 --> 00:39:17.040
that you were talking about a few minutes ago.

937
00:39:17.040 --> 00:39:19.350
In immersing myself in your world,

938
00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:22.890
it's left me very reflective on kind of my own upbringing.

939
00:39:22.890 --> 00:39:24.570
And I'm gonna resist the temptation

940
00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:26.970
to make this too much about a personal, (laughs)

941
00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:27.963
session with you.

942
00:39:29.220 --> 00:39:31.620
But I do wanna share this one thing,

943
00:39:31.620 --> 00:39:34.650
'cause I think it's a illustrative of the many points

944
00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:38.010
that you're underscoring here,

945
00:39:38.010 --> 00:39:40.980
which is that I grew up with a mother

946
00:39:40.980 --> 00:39:45.980
who suffered at least two pretty acute traumas

947
00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:47.550
that I can point my finger to,

948
00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:50.070
one being the passing of her father

949
00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:52.110
when she was quite young in college.

950
00:39:52.110 --> 00:39:54.240
And by all accounts, he was an amazing human.

951
00:39:54.240 --> 00:39:55.680
He died before I was born.

952
00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:56.790
I'm named after him.

953
00:39:56.790 --> 00:39:59.370
He was a very important figure in her life.

954
00:39:59.370 --> 00:40:01.050
And then subsequently

955
00:40:01.050 --> 00:40:04.680
her brother passing away tragically in a car accident

956
00:40:04.680 --> 00:40:06.360
when she must have been,
Oh my goodness.

957
00:40:06.360 --> 00:40:09.657
30, late thirties, maybe 40, something like that.

958
00:40:09.657 --> 00:40:13.000
And I have a vivid memory as a very young person

959
00:40:14.340 --> 00:40:19.110
experiencing her grief and trauma as a result of that.

960
00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:21.660
But she's also somebody who didn't grow up

961
00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:26.660
in an environment where therapy and these modalities

962
00:40:27.360 --> 00:40:30.240
were kind of something that you do, right?

963
00:40:30.240 --> 00:40:35.240
So she repressed these experiences, compartmentalized them,

964
00:40:35.820 --> 00:40:38.670
and then just did her best to move on.

965
00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:43.650
And over time, as my sister and I kind of grew up,

966
00:40:43.650 --> 00:40:45.810
it became very clear, and I wasn't aware of this

967
00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:48.510
until many, many years later,

968
00:40:48.510 --> 00:40:51.420
the extent to which we were raised in an environment

969
00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:54.480
where we were told in no uncertain terms

970
00:40:54.480 --> 00:40:57.510
that the world is unsafe, and it's scary,

971
00:40:57.510 --> 00:40:59.700
and risk is unacceptable,

972
00:40:59.700 --> 00:41:01.830
and you need to do the secure thing.

973
00:41:01.830 --> 00:41:06.090
And there was a sort of pressure associated with that.

974
00:41:06.090 --> 00:41:10.530
Like the way to be safe and secure is to excel in education

975
00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:13.800
or to kind of distinguish yourself and perform.

976
00:41:13.800 --> 00:41:17.700
And then, you know, thereafter pursue a career

977
00:41:17.700 --> 00:41:19.770
that is very safe, right?
Yes.

978
00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:24.770
And so it was an environment of fear, of catastrophizing,

979
00:41:25.020 --> 00:41:27.990
of the shoe is about to drop, it's right around the corner,

980
00:41:27.990 --> 00:41:29.910
and we should all be very afraid.

981
00:41:29.910 --> 00:41:34.620
And you know, in kind of recognizing this,

982
00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:38.670
I've experienced a roller coaster of emotions

983
00:41:38.670 --> 00:41:39.503
over the years.

984
00:41:39.503 --> 00:41:41.700
Anger, mostly resentment.

985
00:41:41.700 --> 00:41:43.710
Like why couldn't she have gotten help?

986
00:41:43.710 --> 00:41:46.560
What would've happened had she gotten the help

987
00:41:46.560 --> 00:41:47.970
that she needed?

988
00:41:47.970 --> 00:41:49.950
And I have to practice a tremendous amount

989
00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:53.490
of contrary action to forgive her,

990
00:41:53.490 --> 00:41:56.070
to forgive myself for the behaviors

991
00:41:56.070 --> 00:41:57.690
and the decisions that I made

992
00:41:57.690 --> 00:41:59.970
as a result of that upbringing,

993
00:41:59.970 --> 00:42:02.670
and to practice loving kindness.

994
00:42:02.670 --> 00:42:07.260
Because in truth, those traumas led her

995
00:42:07.260 --> 00:42:12.260
to become a person who was from her own heart center,

996
00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:14.070
you know, love for her children,

997
00:42:14.070 --> 00:42:15.330
was just trying to protect her kids.

998
00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:18.000
Like she was doing what she thought was in our best interest

999
00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:21.330
because she didn't want that to happen to us.

1000
00:42:21.330 --> 00:42:24.030
Yes.
But the extent to which

1001
00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:26.220
that kind of metastasized

1002
00:42:26.220 --> 00:42:31.220
and led to outcomes that none of us wanted is severe.

1003
00:42:32.370 --> 00:42:34.200
And I would have to imagine, you know,

1004
00:42:34.200 --> 00:42:36.840
in talking about the pervasiveness of trauma,

1005
00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:39.750
that this is a very garden variety story

1006
00:42:39.750 --> 00:42:41.640
that is relatable to a lot of people,

1007
00:42:41.640 --> 00:42:44.070
and I'm not trying to, you know,

1008
00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:45.480
shove my mom under the bus or anything.

1009
00:42:45.480 --> 00:42:47.370
I love my parents, they're fantastic.

1010
00:42:47.370 --> 00:42:50.670
And like all of us, we are all doing our best.

1011
00:42:50.670 --> 00:42:53.370
And I think what I wanna kind of land on here

1012
00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:57.450
is that I've had to literally, you know,

1013
00:42:57.450 --> 00:43:00.630
move emotional and mental mountains over many years

1014
00:43:00.630 --> 00:43:03.540
to transcend these behavior patterns,

1015
00:43:03.540 --> 00:43:05.610
to make peace with all of this,

1016
00:43:05.610 --> 00:43:10.440
and find new strategies and models for living,

1017
00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:13.050
and I've succeeded in doing that.

1018
00:43:13.050 --> 00:43:18.050
And yet, as a parent of four kids, I will still find myself,

1019
00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:21.900
like so much of my parenting

1020
00:43:21.900 --> 00:43:25.230
is in opposition to that, right?

1021
00:43:25.230 --> 00:43:27.720
Almost too much to the other direction.

1022
00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:30.630
And yet, in less conscious moments,

1023
00:43:30.630 --> 00:43:33.180
I'll find myself repeating those behavior patterns.

1024
00:43:33.180 --> 00:43:34.927
And it's my wife who's like,

1025
00:43:34.927 --> 00:43:36.090
"You're doing that thing again."

1026
00:43:36.090 --> 00:43:39.210
And she catches me, then I catch myself.

1027
00:43:39.210 --> 00:43:42.870
So it's this autopilot that is so entrenched.

1028
00:43:42.870 --> 00:43:44.880
Yes.
Over many, many, many years,

1029
00:43:44.880 --> 00:43:46.890
that even myself being someone

1030
00:43:46.890 --> 00:43:49.608
who really wants to excavate all of this

1031
00:43:49.608 --> 00:43:53.250
and overcome it, it still persists.

1032
00:43:53.250 --> 00:43:56.190
It's still there, it's an ongoing thing.

1033
00:43:56.190 --> 00:43:57.023
Yes.

1034
00:43:57.023 --> 00:43:58.920
I think the story you just told is,

1035
00:43:58.920 --> 00:44:01.950
it's so powerful an example,

1036
00:44:01.950 --> 00:44:06.466
precisely because of its, essentially, its normalcy, right?

1037
00:44:06.466 --> 00:44:08.790
I mean, you think about how someone might just

1038
00:44:08.790 --> 00:44:09.870
tell your mother's history of like,

1039
00:44:09.870 --> 00:44:11.250
well she lost a parent early

1040
00:44:11.250 --> 00:44:13.020
and lost a brother in a car accident.

