because there then appears to be no better answer, right?

1295
00:54:40.620 --> 00:54:44.730
And I think the way to come at trauma and addiction

1296
00:54:44.730 --> 00:54:49.410
is to hold forth the truth, and the hopeful truth,

1297
00:54:49.410 --> 00:54:52.530
that no, there is something way better

1298
00:54:52.530 --> 00:54:54.000
than short term soothing.

1299
00:54:54.000 --> 00:54:58.260
In fact, there are ways of coming at it that takes away,

1300
00:54:58.260 --> 00:55:00.450
can take away the desperation

1301
00:55:00.450 --> 00:55:02.520
that makes the short term soothing

1302
00:55:02.520 --> 00:55:04.323
at some points irresistible.

1303
00:55:05.220 --> 00:55:06.053
Does that make,

1304
00:55:06.053 --> 00:55:07.440
does that sound reasonable?
No, it does, it does.

1305
00:55:07.440 --> 00:55:10.710
I'm trying to, you know, understand the relationship

1306
00:55:10.710 --> 00:55:15.710
between confronting this unresolved trauma

1307
00:55:16.710 --> 00:55:19.230
and how that plays out in terms of

1308
00:55:19.230 --> 00:55:22.380
building a foundation of long term sobriety.

1309
00:55:22.380 --> 00:55:24.870
And, you know, I repeat this often on the show,

1310
00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:27.450
but I'm, you know, I got sober in 12-step,

1311
00:55:27.450 --> 00:55:30.270
I'm very much a traditionalist in that modality

1312
00:55:30.270 --> 00:55:34.860
and that kind of toolbox and how, you know,

1313
00:55:34.860 --> 00:55:39.860
that modality relates to a psychiatric modality

1314
00:55:40.050 --> 00:55:41.970
that is related to trauma,

1315
00:55:41.970 --> 00:55:43.860
like where those things intersect

1316
00:55:43.860 --> 00:55:46.290
and where perhaps they're divergent.

1317
00:55:46.290 --> 00:55:47.310
Yeah.

1318
00:55:47.310 --> 00:55:49.680
So maybe one way of coming at that

1319
00:55:49.680 --> 00:55:54.680
is actually approaching and addressing trauma

1320
00:55:55.710 --> 00:55:59.790
can take away one of the most miserable

1321
00:55:59.790 --> 00:56:03.240
and destructive conditions humans can be in,

1322
00:56:03.240 --> 00:56:06.030
which is a damned if you, do damned if you don't, right?

1323
00:56:06.030 --> 00:56:07.743
Like I feel awful.

1324
00:56:09.060 --> 00:56:09.893
I feel terrible.

1325
00:56:09.893 --> 00:56:10.920
I wanna feel better,

1326
00:56:10.920 --> 00:56:12.753
and there's no way out of this.

1327
00:56:13.650 --> 00:56:16.560
So I can go try and do something about it,

1328
00:56:16.560 --> 00:56:18.540
but all the things I do about it,

1329
00:56:18.540 --> 00:56:21.240
which might sometimes be the use of substances,

1330
00:56:21.240 --> 00:56:23.730
make things worse, but there's no other,

1331
00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:24.870
there's nothing else to do about it.

1332
00:56:24.870 --> 00:56:27.270
There's no other way to make it better, right?

1333
00:56:27.270 --> 00:56:28.770
And when we have

1334
00:56:28.770 --> 00:56:30.630
what sometimes is called a central conflict,

1335
00:56:30.630 --> 00:56:33.150
or a damned if you do, damned if you don't,

1336
00:56:33.150 --> 00:56:36.540
we can't stand that as human beings, right?

1337
00:56:36.540 --> 00:56:40.470
We feel we're confronted with helplessness and hopelessness.

1338
00:56:40.470 --> 00:56:45.210
And the idea is then to come at that therapeutically,

1339
00:56:45.210 --> 00:56:47.730
in a way, like, put words to that

1340
00:56:47.730 --> 00:56:51.270
in a way that takes away the dilemma, right?

1341
00:56:51.270 --> 00:56:52.950
It's not damned if you do, damned if you don't,

1342
00:56:52.950 --> 00:56:56.730
there's a third path, there's a route through it, right?

1343
00:56:56.730 --> 00:56:58.673
In some ways, I don't know if you're familiar,

1344
00:56:58.673 --> 00:57:01.020
there's a psychiatrist named Lance Dodes

1345
00:57:01.020 --> 00:57:02.010
who's at Harvard,

1346
00:57:02.010 --> 00:57:04.380
who wrote a book called "The Heart of Addiction,"

1347
00:57:04.380 --> 00:57:08.790
and he is basically writing how traumatic circumstances

1348
00:57:08.790 --> 00:57:12.330
promoted relapse to addictive behaviors.

1349
00:57:12.330 --> 00:57:14.970
And I think it was a long time ago that he wrote that book,

1350
00:57:14.970 --> 00:57:18.330
and I think it captures the concept that even if a person,

1351
00:57:18.330 --> 00:57:22.080
say, has gotten themselves back into a healthier state,

1352
00:57:22.080 --> 00:57:25.050
unless they've resolved that central conflict,

1353
00:57:25.050 --> 00:57:26.430
something can happen that makes them

1354
00:57:26.430 --> 00:57:28.590
feel again like they felt before.

1355
00:57:28.590 --> 00:57:30.630
It's not really a resolution to the state,

1356
00:57:30.630 --> 00:57:32.430
so they can go back to the state,

1357
00:57:32.430 --> 00:57:35.790
and that that was telegraphing so many of the relapses

1358
00:57:35.790 --> 00:57:37.410
that he would see in his practice.

1359
00:57:37.410 --> 00:57:39.670
And I think that there's an immense truth to that.

1360
00:57:39.670 --> 00:57:42.330
I mean, on the one hand,

1361
00:57:42.330 --> 00:57:44.170
addiction is a neurobiological illness, right?

1362
00:57:44.170 --> 00:57:47.760
There are receptors that are operating in divergent manners

1363
00:57:47.760 --> 00:57:49.800
and they're, you know, they're firing in ways

1364
00:57:49.800 --> 00:57:51.330
that sort of demand reinforcement.

1365
00:57:51.330 --> 00:57:53.460
And there's all sorts of things going on there,

1366
00:57:53.460 --> 00:57:55.830
but that doesn't mean that that's necessarily

1367
00:57:55.830 --> 00:57:56.663
at the heart of it.

1368
00:57:56.663 --> 00:58:01.020
Like we can get to the point of that neurobiological state,

1369
00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:05.850
but it arises, often, from a psychological dilemma,

1370
00:58:05.850 --> 00:58:09.600
and the psychological dilemma that arises from trauma.

1371
00:58:09.600 --> 00:58:10.590
Does that?

1372
00:58:10.590 --> 00:58:13.590
Yeah, no, it makes a lot of sense.

1373
00:58:13.590 --> 00:58:14.883
It makes a lot of sense.

1374
00:58:15.810 --> 00:58:20.810
In healing, confronting, healing, that historic trauma,

1375
00:58:23.310 --> 00:58:24.900
I see a path forward

1376
00:58:24.900 --> 00:58:26.970
towards building that foundation of sobriety,

1377
00:58:26.970 --> 00:58:28.950
but I feel like it's just one,

1378
00:58:28.950 --> 00:58:32.100
there are many other tools that still come into play.

1379
00:58:32.100 --> 00:58:33.870
Sure.
Like it's not as simplistic

1380
00:58:33.870 --> 00:58:37.288
as deal with the trauma, then you're good.

1381
00:58:37.288 --> 00:58:38.121
Right, it's-
And I, sorry,

1382
00:58:38.121 --> 00:58:40.980
that's the one thing that I kind of like bristle back at,

1383
00:58:40.980 --> 00:58:42.810
because it's not a binary.
Oh, sure.

1384
00:58:42.810 --> 00:58:44.040
Absolutely.

1385
00:58:44.040 --> 00:58:47.460
We want everything that we can have in our favor,

1386
00:58:47.460 --> 00:58:50.430
to be healthy and happy in our favor, right?

1387
00:58:50.430 --> 00:58:53.220
So addressing trauma,

1388
00:58:53.220 --> 00:58:56.340
and understanding that comes through trauma

1389
00:58:56.340 --> 00:58:58.650
that can resolve those damned if you do,

1390
00:58:58.650 --> 00:59:01.260
damned if you don't dilemmas is important,

1391
00:59:01.260 --> 00:59:03.660
but it's just one part of a structure

1392
00:59:03.660 --> 00:59:08.660
that a person can put around themselves to engender health,

1393
00:59:09.120 --> 00:59:14.120
you know, self-awareness, well-informed life choices,

1394
00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:18.600
reflectiveness, choices about the who, what,

1395
00:59:18.600 --> 00:59:20.910
when, where of self-care.

1396
00:59:20.910 --> 00:59:23.430
There's so much more to it,

1397
00:59:23.430 --> 00:59:26.070
but a central heuristic to understanding it

1398
00:59:26.070 --> 00:59:29.190
and navigating it would say

1399
00:59:29.190 --> 00:59:33.360
you can't expect a person to do that,

1400
00:59:33.360 --> 00:59:35.370
and to have the odds in their favor

1401
00:59:35.370 --> 00:59:36.780
of doing it successfully,

1402
00:59:36.780 --> 00:59:39.330
if there's a central conflict inside of them

1403
00:59:39.330 --> 00:59:41.340
that is still spinning off a damned if you do,

1404
00:59:41.340 --> 00:59:42.390
damned if you don't,
Sure.

1405
00:59:42.390 --> 00:59:44.700
or all the reflexes we were talking about the trauma,

1406
00:59:44.700 --> 00:59:46.260
of like, "Oh, nothing will ever go right for me,"

1407
00:59:46.260 --> 00:59:48.270
or "It's never gonna be okay in the long run."

1408
00:59:48.270 --> 00:59:50.790
Like, how do you build that structure around you

1409
00:59:50.790 --> 00:59:52.740
with that core belief?

1410
00:59:52.740 --> 00:59:53.940
So we wanna come,

1411
00:59:53.940 --> 00:59:57.510
we wanna make sure there is no core belief like that

1412
00:59:57.510 --> 00:59:58.590
when the person is then

1413
00:59:58.590 --> 01:00:00.630
building the healthy structure around them.

1414
01:00:00.630 --> 01:00:02.940
Yeah, no, that makes a lot of sense.

1415
01:00:02.940 --> 01:00:04.500
That makes a lot of sense.

1416
01:00:04.500 --> 01:00:07.557
What are some of the other ways beyond addiction

1417
01:00:07.557 --> 01:00:10.383
that trauma manifests?

1418
01:00:11.250 --> 01:00:12.210
Sure.

1419
01:00:12.210 --> 01:00:14.310
So if we think about the hallmarks

1420
01:00:14.310 --> 01:00:18.030
of a post-trauma syndrome, which often gets called PTSD,

1421
01:00:18.030 --> 01:00:21.004
but like the word trauma, PTSD gets thrown around

1422
01:00:21.004 --> 01:00:22.210
a lot, right?
Yeah.

1423
01:00:22.210 --> 01:00:23.880
And I think there are limitations.

1424
01:00:23.880 --> 01:00:25.763
I mean, it's a DSM definition, right?

1425
01:00:25.763 --> 01:00:29.910
And the DSM is a book of taxonomy, right?

1426
01:00:29.910 --> 01:00:33.210
It doesn't tell you, it doesn't say what truth is, right?

1427
01:00:33.210 --> 01:00:37.860
So for example, PTSD can be vicarious if it's occupational,

1428
01:00:37.860 --> 01:00:39.270
but not if it's not occupational.

1429
01:00:39.270 --> 01:00:42.150
Like that doesn't make sense to the truth of the brain,

1430
01:00:42.150 --> 01:00:44.910
setting something so arbitrary, right?

1431
01:00:44.910 --> 01:00:46.350
So if we look at it and we say,

1432
01:00:46.350 --> 01:00:48.930
what actually goes on in human beings

1433
01:00:48.930 --> 01:00:51.000
when there's a change in the brain,

1434
01:00:51.000 --> 01:00:53.160
and that change is a consequence of trauma?

1435
01:00:53.160 --> 01:00:53.993
Right?

1436
01:00:53.993 --> 01:00:56.430
And a lot of it is very similar.

1437
01:00:56.430 --> 01:00:58.920
You know, it tracks along with PTSD criteria, right?

1438
01:00:58.920 --> 01:01:00.510
So there's exposure to something,

1439
01:01:00.510 --> 01:01:03.360
whether it's acute, or chronic, or vicarious.

1440
01:01:03.360 --> 01:01:05.520
It is then re-experienced,

1441
01:01:05.520 --> 01:01:07.770
meaning that there are intrusive thoughts about it.

1442
01:01:07.770 --> 01:01:09.450
There are recurrent thoughts about it.

1443
01:01:09.450 --> 01:01:11.430
It's on the person's mind.

1444
01:01:11.430 --> 01:01:14.580
It gets mapped to things they see in the world around them.

1445
01:01:14.580 --> 01:01:16.020
You know, someone who is in an accident

1446
01:01:16.020 --> 01:01:17.160
who sees the car go by

1447
01:01:17.160 --> 01:01:19.560
and imagines the car hits something next, right?

1448
01:01:19.560 --> 01:01:21.383
So it becomes ingrained in the person

1449
01:01:21.383 --> 01:01:24.540
in a way that is a lot of re-experience to it.

1450
01:01:24.540 --> 01:01:28.050
Then there's a shift towards brain mechanisms

1451
01:01:28.050 --> 01:01:30.900
that are more about fear, and vulnerability,

1452
01:01:30.900 --> 01:01:32.430
and vigilance, right?

1453
01:01:32.430 --> 01:01:34.800
So people then become more vigilant

1454
01:01:34.800 --> 01:01:36.720
and want to maybe take less opportunities.

1455
01:01:36.720 --> 01:01:39.540
You know, the idea of like, you know what, I wanna be safe.

1456
01:01:39.540 --> 01:01:42.000
The best way I can be safe is to not leave the house,

1457
01:01:42.000 --> 01:01:43.800
ergo, I'm not leaving the house, right?

1458
01:01:43.800 --> 01:01:46.500
And then like, look at how much of life is cut off, right?

1459
01:01:46.500 --> 01:01:51.270
So the shift in us towards vigilance is negative.

1460
01:01:51.270 --> 01:01:54.330
Then there can be shifts that raise baseline anxiety

1461
01:01:54.330 --> 01:01:55.620
and tension inside of us,

1462
01:01:55.620 --> 01:01:58.440
that lower mood, that impact sleep,

1463
01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:00.990
and that lead to behavioral changes.

1464
01:02:00.990 --> 01:02:04.710
So the idea would be, that's what we're looking for, right?

1465
01:02:04.710 --> 01:02:07.920
Because those are the manifestations of a change

1466
01:02:07.920 --> 01:02:09.840
that has happened in the brain

1467
01:02:09.840 --> 01:02:13.770
that's not a healthy change that we wanna recognize, right?

1468
01:02:13.770 --> 01:02:15.510
Whether it's a clinician recognizing,

1469
01:02:15.510 --> 01:02:17.580
or someone who's being reflective about themselves,

1470
01:02:17.580 --> 01:02:19.140
or someone else in their life.

1471
01:02:19.140 --> 01:02:22.470
We wanna recognize, hey, like, that's a real change.

1472
01:02:22.470 --> 01:02:26.100
And it warrants going in and looking at that

1473
01:02:26.100 --> 01:02:28.560
and trying to fix it, right?

1474
01:02:28.560 --> 01:02:32.943
Trying to change that back to how it was before.

1475
01:02:34.050 --> 01:02:36.630
The sleep impingement one is super interesting, right?

1476
01:02:36.630 --> 01:02:37.830
Yes.
I think a lot of people

1477
01:02:37.830 --> 01:02:40.230
believe, or they're operating under this idea

1478
01:02:40.230 --> 01:02:44.250
that they have a sleep disorder or some form of insomnia,

1479
01:02:44.250 --> 01:02:46.590
when in reality, they're just looping

1480
01:02:46.590 --> 01:02:48.840
these thoughts that are activating

1481
01:02:48.840 --> 01:02:50.190
their brain
Yes.

1482
01:02:50.190 --> 01:02:52.170
and their neurochemistry in a way

1483
01:02:52.170 --> 01:02:53.763
that's just getting in the way of sleep, right?

1484
01:02:53.763 --> 01:02:54.780
Yes.
It's sort of like

1485
01:02:54.780 --> 01:02:56.970
these patterns that, you know,
Yes.

1486
01:02:56.970 --> 01:03:01.590
are sort of hard to overcome or repress.

1487
01:03:01.590 --> 01:03:05.340
I cannot say a strong enough "yes" to that.

1488
01:03:05.340 --> 01:03:07.230
And over the years,

1489
01:03:07.230 --> 01:03:09.630
amongst the conditions I have seen treated

1490
01:03:09.630 --> 01:03:13.470
the absolute worst in mental health treatment,

1491
01:03:13.470 --> 01:03:15.270
and indeed, in general medical treatment,

1492
01:03:15.270 --> 01:03:16.680
are sleep problems,

1493
01:03:16.680 --> 01:03:17.823
because, you know,

1494
01:03:19.830 --> 01:03:23.070
we're living in an era of rapid fire

1495
01:03:23.070 --> 01:03:24.480
throughput medicine, right?

1496
01:03:24.480 --> 01:03:26.670
And as I often will describe it as like,

1497
01:03:26.670 --> 01:03:29.010
polish the hood if there's a problem in the engine, right?

1498
01:03:29.010 --> 01:03:29.967
Then things look better for a little bit

1499
01:03:29.967 --> 01:03:32.670
and we can call it good and shuffle the person on

1500
01:03:32.670 --> 01:03:33.540
down the road.

1501
01:03:33.540 --> 01:03:36.660
And we see that so strongly with sleep,

1502
01:03:36.660 --> 01:03:39.450
where we're like, anyone who has a sleep problem is,

1503
01:03:39.450 --> 01:03:40.290
well, they're not sleeping,

1504
01:03:40.290 --> 01:03:42.630
so they need a sleeping medicine, right?

1505
01:03:42.630 --> 01:03:46.590
And it's like, it's so overly simplistic,

1506
01:03:46.590 --> 01:03:49.230
and it doesn't honor at all

1507
01:03:49.230 --> 01:03:51.210
that there are things that can be in our mind

1508
01:03:51.210 --> 01:03:52.770
in ways that are ruminative, right?

1509
01:03:52.770 --> 01:03:55.620
These deep distress systems in our brains

1510
01:03:55.620 --> 01:03:58.440
that feel so unsafe that they just have us ruminating,

1511
01:03:58.440 --> 01:03:59.940
going over and over.

1512
01:03:59.940 --> 01:04:02.027
And I cannot tell you how many times

1513
01:04:02.027 --> 01:04:05.340
I have seen a person who quote, unquote, "can't sleep."

1514
01:04:05.340 --> 01:04:09.450
And, oh, they've been on 4, 6, 10 sleeping medicines.

1515
01:04:09.450 --> 01:04:10.283
Oh, my goodness, like they can't sleep.

1516
01:04:10.283 --> 01:04:12.000
And it's like, right,

1517
01:04:12.000 --> 01:04:14.790
they don't have a sleeping system problem, right?

1518
01:04:14.790 --> 01:04:15.990
They have a trauma problem.

1519
01:04:15.990 --> 01:04:17.430
They have a rumination problem.

1520
01:04:17.430 --> 01:04:20.250
And if we look at it through that lens,

1521
01:04:20.250 --> 01:04:21.720
which is the lens of truth,

1522
01:04:21.720 --> 01:04:23.430
of what's actually going on in them,

1523
01:04:23.430 --> 01:04:25.050
we can resolve that problem.

1524
01:04:25.050 --> 01:04:27.000
Whereas you'll never resolve that problem

1525
01:04:27.000 --> 01:04:28.020
with a sleeping medicine,

1526
01:04:28.020 --> 01:04:30.690
because that's not where the problem is.

1527
01:04:30.690 --> 01:04:32.820
But in a system of reflexive medicine

1528
01:04:32.820 --> 01:04:34.560
and overburdened physicians,

1529
01:04:34.560 --> 01:04:36.690
especially the primary care physicians,

1530
01:04:36.690 --> 01:04:38.040
where people are just overburdened,

1531
01:04:38.040 --> 01:04:39.480
so of course they're looking for like,

1532
01:04:39.480 --> 01:04:40.980
okay, like what, you're not sleeping?

1533
01:04:40.980 --> 01:04:42.000
Let me give you sleeping medicine.

1534
01:04:42.000 --> 01:04:44.130
Because they have 11 minutes with the person, right?

1535
01:04:44.130 --> 01:04:45.540
And how else are they gonna do it?

1536
01:04:45.540 --> 01:04:47.160
And I think we,

1537
01:04:47.160 --> 01:04:51.090
there's so much misery and sometimes death in people.

1538
01:04:51.090 --> 01:04:54.510
So much needless suffering and so much needless cost

1539
01:04:54.510 --> 01:04:55.800
because our healthcare system

1540
01:04:55.800 --> 01:04:59.280
has become focused on throughput, not thinking.

1541
01:04:59.280 --> 01:05:02.431
And then we impose that upon the people

1542
01:05:02.431 --> 01:05:03.780
who are working in the system.

1543
01:05:03.780 --> 01:05:05.730
And we impose that upon all of us

1544
01:05:05.730 --> 01:05:07.620
who are trying to get healthier within the system.

1545
01:05:07.620 --> 01:05:09.060
And how can we be surprised

1546
01:05:09.060 --> 01:05:11.580
that we often end up with dumbed down answers

1547
01:05:11.580 --> 01:05:12.476
that don't work?
Yeah.

1548
01:05:12.476 --> 01:05:13.600
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1549
01:05:13.600 --> 01:05:16.440
Yeah, I would venture to imagine

1550
01:05:16.440 --> 01:05:21.000
that the rumination factor is at root

1551
01:05:21.000 --> 01:05:23.850
in the vast majority of people

1552
01:05:23.850 --> 01:05:26.640
who would claim to have sleep disorders.

1553
01:05:26.640 --> 01:05:29.640
I believe that to be true as well,

1554
01:05:29.640 --> 01:05:30.720
to the best of my knowledge,

1555
01:05:30.720 --> 01:05:32.460
there's no data about it.

1556
01:05:32.460 --> 01:05:34.080
I don't know if there ever will be,

1557
01:05:34.080 --> 01:05:37.920
but it's just so, what I say, clinically evident, right?

1558
01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:39.480
Something where you just sit with a person,

1559
01:05:39.480 --> 01:05:40.801
you just know what that is.

1560
01:05:40.801 --> 01:05:43.170
Then you have the proof of concept, right?

1561
01:05:43.170 --> 01:05:47.040
You come at it not by giving them the ninth sleep medicine,

1562
01:05:47.040 --> 01:05:50.340
but by coming at the reasons behind the rumination,

1563
01:05:50.340 --> 01:05:52.820
and sometimes coming at that pharmacologically, right?

1564
01:05:52.820 --> 01:05:54.630
So you try and shut down the rumination

1565
01:05:54.630 --> 01:05:57.690
while then working with words to solve the problems,

1566
01:05:57.690 --> 01:05:58.857
or you just come at it with the words,

1567
01:05:58.857 --> 01:06:01.899
and you see, oh, that made that better.

1568
01:06:01.899 --> 01:06:03.783
So there's the proof of concept,

1569
01:06:04.620 --> 01:06:07.110
which is obvious when a person presents

1570
01:06:07.110 --> 01:06:08.340
with that kind of rumination.

1571
01:06:08.340 --> 01:06:09.457
Like, they don't say like,

1572
01:06:09.457 --> 01:06:10.547
"Oh, I just can't sleep," or,

1573
01:06:10.547 --> 01:06:11.940
"Well, I'm tired, but I can't."

1574
01:06:11.940 --> 01:06:14.430
No, they're telling you what's going on in their brain

1575
01:06:14.430 --> 01:06:17.130
and it's scary, and miserable, and vulnerable.

1576
01:06:17.130 --> 01:06:20.505
And it comes to the fore around bedtime, right?

1577
01:06:20.505 --> 01:06:21.810
Like it's just part of how our brains

1578
01:06:21.810 --> 01:06:23.130
start relaxing a little bit

1579
01:06:23.130 --> 01:06:24.690
that lets more of this in,

1580
01:06:24.690 --> 01:06:26.910
because we're less thinking about other things.

1581
01:06:26.910 --> 01:06:30.453
And in many cases it's just so obvious and predictable,

1582
01:06:31.530 --> 01:06:33.270
but the system doesn't identify it.

1583
01:06:33.270 --> 01:06:34.530
Yeah.

1584
01:06:34.530 --> 01:06:38.790
In my mind that that serves as sort of a micro example

1585
01:06:38.790 --> 01:06:43.350
of these autopilot behaviors that we're semi-unconscious of,

1586
01:06:43.350 --> 01:06:46.980
but a very common, you know, more macro pattern

1587
01:06:46.980 --> 01:06:50.700
would be the example of the person

1588
01:06:50.700 --> 01:06:54.870
who is in an abusive romantic relationship.

1589
01:06:54.870 --> 01:06:58.230
That relationship ends, they're in another relationship,

1590
01:06:58.230 --> 01:07:01.410
the same pattern emerges again, and again, and again.

1591
01:07:01.410 --> 01:07:04.440
And you know, they vary in the extent

1592
01:07:04.440 --> 01:07:06.240
to which they're even aware

1593
01:07:06.240 --> 01:07:08.430
that they're repeating this pattern,

1594
01:07:08.430 --> 01:07:10.440
and there's a sense of powerlessness.

1595
01:07:10.440 --> 01:07:12.960
Like you're continuing to seek out,

1596
01:07:12.960 --> 01:07:14.790
because of something that happened to you

1597
01:07:14.790 --> 01:07:16.561
a long, long time ago,

1598
01:07:16.561 --> 01:07:21.561
you're recreating this situation in your life.

1599
01:07:21.660 --> 01:07:23.520
Yes, yes.

1600
01:07:23.520 --> 01:07:28.350
I find it like really amazing

1601
01:07:28.350 --> 01:07:33.030
how often this happens in us, in human beings.

1602
01:07:33.030 --> 01:07:34.950
It gets called a repetition compulsion.

1603
01:07:34.950 --> 01:07:37.890
Like we can put whatever words we want to it,

1604
01:07:37.890 --> 01:07:41.220
but it's rooted in this, like,

1605
01:07:41.220 --> 01:07:45.360
I think amazing fact that, like,

1606
01:07:45.360 --> 01:07:48.990
for the vast majority of my life, I wasn't aware of,

1607
01:07:48.990 --> 01:07:51.690
which is that, okay, there are logic systems in our brain

1608
01:07:51.690 --> 01:07:53.550
and there are affective, or emotion systems,

1609
01:07:53.550 --> 01:07:55.200
what's called the limbic system in our brain.

1610
01:07:55.200 --> 01:07:57.690
So there's logic and there's emotion, basically.

1611
01:07:57.690 --> 01:08:02.690
And emotion overrides logic like all the time, right?

1612
01:08:03.630 --> 01:08:05.700
If emotion feels strongly about something,

1613
01:08:05.700 --> 01:08:08.163
it always overrides logic.

1614
01:08:09.390 --> 01:08:11.040
But we're not aware of that.

1615
01:08:11.040 --> 01:08:13.380
And we're also not aware that those emotion systems

1616
01:08:13.380 --> 01:08:16.320
in our brain do not care about the clock and the calendar.

1617
01:08:16.320 --> 01:08:18.540
Because people say, well, why, you know,

1618
01:08:18.540 --> 01:08:21.060
if you've had four relationships that have been,

1619
01:08:21.060 --> 01:08:23.130
say, basically the same abusive relationship,

1620
01:08:23.130 --> 01:08:24.510
but the person just had a different name,

1621
01:08:24.510 --> 01:08:25.890
basically the same person.

1622
01:08:25.890 --> 01:08:27.390
Why would you do that again?

1623
01:08:27.390 --> 01:08:30.150
Why would you think you could fix the past

1624
01:08:30.150 --> 01:08:32.280
by doing that same thing in the future

1625
01:08:32.280 --> 01:08:33.930
and gaining some mastery?

1626
01:08:33.930 --> 01:08:35.280
It's the wrong question to ask,

1627
01:08:35.280 --> 01:08:36.510
because that part of the brain

1628
01:08:36.510 --> 01:08:39.780
isn't discerning past from present, right?

1629
01:08:39.780 --> 01:08:43.800
It's all immediate and the brain wants to solve it.

1630
01:08:43.800 --> 01:08:46.380
It wants to release itself from the fear

1631
01:08:46.380 --> 01:08:48.420
and the trauma of what happened in the past

1632
01:08:48.420 --> 01:08:52.020
to correct it by what happens in the present,

1633
01:08:52.020 --> 01:08:54.090
because it's not making those,

1634
01:08:54.090 --> 01:08:55.830
it's not distinguishing like that.

1635
01:08:55.830 --> 01:08:57.600
Which is why people will say, well, how could that be true?

1636
01:08:57.600 --> 01:08:59.220
Like everybody knows you're not gonna fix something

1637
01:08:59.220 --> 01:09:00.540
in the past.

1638
01:09:00.540 --> 01:09:01.373
That's not true.

1639
01:09:01.373 --> 01:09:03.360
The logical parts of our brain know that,

1640
01:09:03.360 --> 01:09:05.400
but when there's highly charged emotion,

1641
01:09:05.400 --> 01:09:07.950
the logical parts of our brain don't matter.

1642
01:09:07.950 --> 01:09:10.590
And unless we honor that in us,

1643
01:09:10.590 --> 01:09:14.040
how are we going to stop repeating the same cycles,

1644
01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:17.070
whether they're individual and psychological,

1645
01:09:17.070 --> 01:09:21.120
or they're aggregate and sociological?

1646
01:09:21.120 --> 01:09:22.470
People say, "Oh, history repeats itself."

1647
01:09:22.470 --> 01:09:24.240
Right, history repeats itself,

1648
01:09:24.240 --> 01:09:26.280
'cause if we're not aware,

1649
01:09:26.280 --> 01:09:29.610
then we do the same things on aggregate scales

1650
01:09:29.610 --> 01:09:32.220
that we do in our own individual lives.

1651
01:09:32.220 --> 01:09:34.890
And we engage in this repetition compulsion

1652
01:09:34.890 --> 01:09:37.230
and you know, everything else that we see out

1653
01:09:37.230 --> 01:09:38.970
play on bigger historical stages.

1654
01:09:38.970 --> 01:09:41.610
So I think what we're talking about now

1655
01:09:41.610 --> 01:09:44.640
is actually intrinsic to the survival of the species.

1656
01:09:44.640 --> 01:09:46.920
And it might be, I know it's a strong thing to say,

1657
01:09:46.920 --> 01:09:48.547
but I really do believe that.

1658
01:09:48.547 --> 01:09:51.180
You know, I think that culture

1659
01:09:51.180 --> 01:09:54.870
is the sociological equivalent of free will.

1660
01:09:54.870 --> 01:09:57.420
Like if we know ourselves and understand ourselves

1661
01:09:57.420 --> 01:10:00.870
as best we can, we exercise free will as best we can.

1662
01:10:00.870 --> 01:10:03.630
But we also establish a culture around us.

1663
01:10:03.630 --> 01:10:07.200
Is it a culture of helping and supporting people?

1664
01:10:07.200 --> 01:10:10.530
Is it a culture of repeating traumatic distress

1665
01:10:10.530 --> 01:10:12.000
over, and over, and over again?

1666
01:10:12.000 --> 01:10:14.460
Like we establish the culture,

1667
01:10:14.460 --> 01:10:18.390
and then the culture, I believe, determines whether or not

1668
01:10:18.390 --> 01:10:21.930
we engage in these big repetition compulsions.

1669
01:10:21.930 --> 01:10:24.270
You know, repetition compulsions of war

1670
01:10:24.270 --> 01:10:28.500
and of lack of self-care to the environment around us.

1671
01:10:28.500 --> 01:10:30.120
And as things get bigger and bigger,

1672
01:10:30.120 --> 01:10:32.010
and we're truly on a world stage,

1673
01:10:32.010 --> 01:10:33.870
for example, with climate change,

1674
01:10:33.870 --> 01:10:36.690
we run the risk that we keep repeating those cycles,

1675
01:10:36.690 --> 01:10:38.640
and you know, we blow ourselves up with nuclear weapons

1676
01:10:38.640 --> 01:10:41.460
or we drive the planet into inhabitability.

1677
01:10:41.460 --> 01:10:45.240
Right, that is the ultimate macro example

1678
01:10:45.240 --> 01:10:47.280
of all of this, right?
Yes.

1679
01:10:47.280 --> 01:10:51.780
But to extend the example of the person

1680
01:10:51.780 --> 01:10:53.460
in the abusive relationship.

1681
01:10:53.460 --> 01:10:55.160
Ostensibly, what you're saying is,

1682
01:10:56.550 --> 01:11:00.360
let's say that person grew up with a father who was abusive.

1683
01:11:00.360 --> 01:11:04.020
So unconsciously, they seek out partners

1684
01:11:04.020 --> 01:11:06.420
that mimic their father's behavior.

1685
01:11:06.420 --> 01:11:07.253
Yes.

1686
01:11:07.253 --> 01:11:08.730
Because what they're attempting to do,

1687
01:11:08.730 --> 01:11:11.790
without their free will or awareness,

1688
01:11:11.790 --> 01:11:13.380
is to fix the past.

1689
01:11:13.380 --> 01:11:14.790
Like if I get into this relationship

1690
01:11:14.790 --> 01:11:16.890
with someone similar to my father

1691
01:11:16.890 --> 01:11:19.470
and I can make it a healthy relationship,

1692
01:11:19.470 --> 01:11:22.140
that will rewrite how I feel about what happened

1693
01:11:22.140 --> 01:11:23.550
when I was a child.
Yes.

1694
01:11:23.550 --> 01:11:24.663
Yes, yes.
Yeah.

1695
01:11:25.950 --> 01:11:27.210
That one, I understand.

1696
01:11:27.210 --> 01:11:29.130
The one that's harder to understand

1697
01:11:29.130 --> 01:11:34.130
is the sort of converse example of the person who suffered,

1698
01:11:36.300 --> 01:11:40.650
let's say a young boy was sexually molested

1699
01:11:40.650 --> 01:11:42.090
as a young person.

1700
01:11:42.090 --> 01:11:45.870
That person quite often grows up to be somebody

1701
01:11:45.870 --> 01:11:50.870
who then perpetrates that same abuse on other people.

1702
01:11:50.940 --> 01:11:53.910
So what is that person unconsciously

1703
01:11:53.910 --> 01:11:55.920
or consciously attempting to do?

1704
01:11:55.920 --> 01:11:57.660
Like why did those people end up

1705
01:11:57.660 --> 01:12:02.660
becoming the perpetrators of the harm that was so traumatic?

1706
01:12:02.730 --> 01:12:03.563
Right.

1707
01:12:03.563 --> 01:12:04.830
So again, great question.

1708
01:12:04.830 --> 01:12:08.280
And so there are different ways that our brains

1709
01:12:08.280 --> 01:12:12.600
will try and solve the things that terrify us, right?

1710
01:12:12.600 --> 01:12:14.070
Those central conflicts.

1711
01:12:14.070 --> 01:12:16.560
I must have a good,

1712
01:12:16.560 --> 01:12:18.030
I must have a romantic relationship

1713
01:12:18.030 --> 01:12:19.800
with someone who loves me, right?

1714
01:12:19.800 --> 01:12:21.330
But I can't,

1715
01:12:21.330 --> 01:12:22.477
because I wasn't even loved by my father, right?

1716
01:12:22.477 --> 01:12:24.480
And then, okay, well the solution state

1717
01:12:24.480 --> 01:12:26.550
is the repetition, right?

1718
01:12:26.550 --> 01:12:28.530
That's one aspect.

1719
01:12:28.530 --> 01:12:31.050
That's one way that we can come at it.

1720
01:12:31.050 --> 01:12:36.050
Another way can be identification with the aggressor, right?

1721
01:12:36.420 --> 01:12:41.420
That being the recipient of sexual abuse is terrifying.

1722
01:12:42.390 --> 01:12:43.770
And when people talk about it,

1723
01:12:43.770 --> 01:12:45.750
there are sensations, and thoughts, and feelings

1724
01:12:45.750 --> 01:12:48.450
that are so inappropriate to the age

1725
01:12:48.450 --> 01:12:51.990
and the situation that they're wildly confusing

1726
01:12:51.990 --> 01:12:54.060
and frightening, right?

1727
01:12:54.060 --> 01:12:57.870
So there's such a strong sense of helplessness

1728
01:12:57.870 --> 01:13:01.260
that the person then sometimes identifies

1729
01:13:01.260 --> 01:13:03.990
with feeling powerful and feeling safe.

1730
01:13:03.990 --> 01:13:07.440
It would be safer to feel like the abuser

1731
01:13:07.440 --> 01:13:09.960
than the person who is being abused.

1732
01:13:09.960 --> 01:13:13.410
And there's a there's a truism that I believe,

1733
01:13:13.410 --> 01:13:14.250
I think this goes back

1734
01:13:14.250 --> 01:13:16.020
like over a hundred years in the field,

1735
01:13:16.020 --> 01:13:18.930
but I believe it to be absolutely true,

1736
01:13:18.930 --> 01:13:21.750
which is there's no internal victim

1737
01:13:21.750 --> 01:13:23.730
without an internal persecutor.

1738
01:13:23.730 --> 01:13:27.300
So if someone internalizes being victimized,

1739
01:13:27.300 --> 01:13:29.130
you know, not like, oh, someone did something to me

1740
01:13:29.130 --> 01:13:31.410
and I was victimized under the eyes of the law, right?

1741
01:13:31.410 --> 01:13:32.880
But like, I am a victim.

1742
01:13:32.880 --> 01:13:37.050
I'm someone who people can do awful things to, right?

1743
01:13:37.050 --> 01:13:38.340
Like that's me, I'm a victim.

1744
01:13:38.340 --> 01:13:41.550
People also internalize the persecutor.

1745
01:13:41.550 --> 01:13:43.890
And very often what they're doing then

1746
01:13:43.890 --> 01:13:46.020
is persecuting themselves, right?

1747
01:13:46.020 --> 01:13:47.640
So they're internalizing that person

1748
01:13:47.640 --> 01:13:49.140
who told them they were worthless,

1749
01:13:49.140 --> 01:13:50.610
is still telling them they're worthless

1750
01:13:50.610 --> 01:13:52.710
'cause they took that person into themselves.

1751
01:13:52.710 --> 01:13:55.920
But another way or direction that can go

1752
01:13:55.920 --> 01:13:58.170
is internalizing the persecutor

1753
01:13:58.170 --> 01:14:00.180
and identifying with the persecutor

1754
01:14:00.180 --> 01:14:02.100
in order to feel safer,

1755
01:14:02.100 --> 01:14:03.960
which is what can then lead

1756
01:14:03.960 --> 01:14:07.350
to perpetrating the same crime

1757
01:14:07.350 --> 01:14:08.780
that was perpetrated upon that person.

1758
01:14:08.780 --> 01:14:10.230
So that person then goes out

1759
01:14:10.230 --> 01:14:12.280
and tells other people they're worthless.

1760
01:14:13.170 --> 01:14:15.210
The person who was told they were worthless

1761
01:14:15.210 --> 01:14:17.760
then repeats that pattern

1762
01:14:17.760 --> 01:14:19.200
by trying to, you know,
Right.

1763
01:14:19.200 --> 01:14:21.120
basically becoming the perpetrator

1764
01:14:21.120 --> 01:14:25.230
as a way of reconciling the victimhood.

1765
01:14:25.230 --> 01:14:27.330
Yeah, and often not in a way of saying,

1766
01:14:27.330 --> 01:14:30.510
oh, I'll, either unconsciously or consciously,

1767
01:14:30.510 --> 01:14:31.890
I'll feel better about myself

1768
01:14:31.890 --> 01:14:34.440
by making someone else feel the way I felt.

1769
01:14:34.440 --> 01:14:36.510
It's generally not that.

1770
01:14:36.510 --> 01:14:38.760
It's if I'm doing this,

1771
01:14:38.760 --> 01:14:42.480
basically if I'm perpetrating crime, then I'm safe.

1772
01:14:42.480 --> 01:14:43.680
I feel powerful, right?

1773
01:14:43.680 --> 01:14:48.330
I have a relief from the terror and the fear inside of me

1774
01:14:48.330 --> 01:14:51.240
of, you know, the ruminative distress of the memories

1775
01:14:51.240 --> 01:14:53.940
and the feelings of having been abused.

1776
01:14:53.940 --> 01:14:55.980
Usually it's in the service

1777
01:14:55.980 --> 01:14:59.430
of the person feeling safe, right?

1778
01:14:59.430 --> 01:15:01.620
Now, there may be a whole set of cognition around that

1779
01:15:01.620 --> 01:15:04.140
that involve knowing that someone else is being hurt,

1780
01:15:04.140 --> 01:15:06.420
you know, of course that's why there's culpability

1781
01:15:06.420 --> 01:15:09.030
and you know, legal culpability for it.

1782
01:15:09.030 --> 01:15:11.700
But ultimately, psychologically, you know,

1783
01:15:11.700 --> 01:15:13.523
people are trying to find safety, right?

1784
01:15:13.523 --> 01:15:16.590
They're trying to find some peace and some safety.

1785
01:15:16.590 --> 01:15:18.960
And we can go about that in some ways

1786
01:15:18.960 --> 01:15:22.380
that can be incredibly damaging and hurtful

1787
01:15:22.380 --> 01:15:25.140
to ourselves and/or others,

1788
01:15:25.140 --> 01:15:27.420
if we don't understand that

1789
01:15:27.420 --> 01:15:29.160
that's what we're trying to achieve in the world.

1790
01:15:29.160 --> 01:15:32.460
The sense of safety, of relief of fear and vulnerability.

1791
01:15:32.460 --> 01:15:34.110
And if we look at that,

1792
01:15:34.110 --> 01:15:36.630
and we wanna understand it and shine the light everywhere

1793
01:15:36.630 --> 01:15:38.730
instead of running from it and hiding from it

1794
01:15:38.730 --> 01:15:40.440
in guilt, and shame, and fear,

1795
01:15:40.440 --> 01:15:42.360
then we navigate that in ways

1796
01:15:42.360 --> 01:15:45.840
that are so much healthier for ourselves,

1797
01:15:45.840 --> 01:15:47.673
and for everyone else too.

1798
01:15:48.780 --> 01:15:51.060
You mentioned PTSD earlier.

1799
01:15:51.060 --> 01:15:54.900
That's a term that is cavalierly kind of tossed about.

1800
01:15:54.900 --> 01:15:57.630
The more that I think about trauma,

1801
01:15:57.630 --> 01:16:01.530
the more I'm confused why this term even exists.

1802
01:16:01.530 --> 01:16:05.610
Like isn't there just trauma and our response to trauma?

1803
01:16:05.610 --> 01:16:08.520
What, if anything, is different about something

1804
01:16:08.520 --> 01:16:13.110
that would be clinically described or diagnosed as PTSD,

1805
01:16:13.110 --> 01:16:15.780
versus somebody else's, you know,

1806
01:16:15.780 --> 01:16:18.930
downstream response to a traumatic event?

1807
01:16:18.930 --> 01:16:20.400
Well, it's a big question

1808
01:16:20.400 --> 01:16:22.740
and I'll try and speak to it kind of concisely.

1809
01:16:22.740 --> 01:16:26.310
That, you know, there need to be criteria

1810
01:16:26.310 --> 01:16:30.180
for diagnoses in order to identify them

1811
01:16:30.180 --> 01:16:31.860
and to communicate between clinicians

1812
01:16:31.860 --> 01:16:34.530
and to enact treatment paradigms, right?

1813
01:16:34.530 --> 01:16:38.490
But what I believe so strongly has happened in the field

1814
01:16:38.490 --> 01:16:41.250
is that mental health, or psychiatry,

1815
01:16:41.250 --> 01:16:42.930
if we're thinking about medicine,

1816
01:16:42.930 --> 01:16:45.750
wants to be like the rest of medicine,

1817
01:16:45.750 --> 01:16:46.620
where it's like very clear,

1818
01:16:46.620 --> 01:16:48.030
do you have a pneumonia or not?

1819
01:16:48.030 --> 01:16:49.020
We can tell that, right?

1820
01:16:49.020 --> 01:16:50.280
Because we're gonna get a chest x-ray

1821
01:16:50.280 --> 01:16:51.420
and we're gonna do blood draws.

1822
01:16:51.420 --> 01:16:52.890
And like, we know you either have a pneumonia

1823
01:16:52.890 --> 01:16:54.000
or you don't, right?

1824
01:16:54.000 --> 01:16:58.083
So we then want these,

1825
01:16:59.160 --> 01:17:01.857
we want a taxonomy that tells us what something is

1826
01:17:01.857 --> 01:17:03.510
and what something isn't.

1827
01:17:03.510 --> 01:17:05.670
And then we do what I think human beings often do,

1828
01:17:05.670 --> 01:17:07.590
is we glorify the taxonomy

1829
01:17:07.590 --> 01:17:10.110
as if the taxonomy is the answer, right?

1830
01:17:10.110 --> 01:17:11.640
Instead of saying, look,

1831
01:17:11.640 --> 01:17:14.370
people can have trauma in all sorts of ways.

1832
01:17:14.370 --> 01:17:16.770
And on the other side of that trauma,

1833
01:17:16.770 --> 01:17:19.710
there can be a whole set of problems, right?

1834
01:17:19.710 --> 01:17:22.680
And the problems often look like this.

1835
01:17:22.680 --> 01:17:24.900
Now, they don't always look like this, right?

1836
01:17:24.900 --> 01:17:26.700
But they often look like this.

1837
01:17:26.700 --> 01:17:29.700
And they come from these changes in us

1838
01:17:29.700 --> 01:17:31.440
that promote these problems.

1839
01:17:31.440 --> 01:17:32.940
That's why you can see mood changes,

1840
01:17:32.940 --> 01:17:35.160
and anxiety changes, and sleep changes,

1841
01:17:35.160 --> 01:17:37.440
because there are all these brain changes.

1842
01:17:37.440 --> 01:17:40.890
But that's less absolute, right?

1843
01:17:40.890 --> 01:17:42.180
Why, because it's real, right?

1844
01:17:42.180 --> 01:17:43.013
It's true.

1845
01:17:43.013 --> 01:17:44.670
It's less absolute than saying,

1846
01:17:44.670 --> 01:17:46.830
look, there's either PTSD or not PTSD,

1847
01:17:46.830 --> 01:17:49.500
and you have to have X criteria in this category

1848
01:17:49.500 --> 01:17:52.904
and Y in that criteria, and it's like, it's false, right?

1849
01:17:52.904 --> 01:17:57.420
But we look at it in a way that glorifies the taxonomy.

1850
01:17:57.420 --> 01:17:58.620
I can remember years ago,

1851
01:17:58.620 --> 01:18:01.200
training a resident who went and saw someone

1852
